.SH NAME
IF77 - Driver for compiling, assembling, and linking Fortran programs for the iPSC\*R/860\fR\fR\fR\fR\& system.
.\" End Summary
.SH SYNOPSIS 
.br
\&\fBif77\fR\fR\& [\fR\fIswitches\fI\fR\&] \fR\fIsourcefile\fI\fR\
\&... 
.SH DESCRIPTION 
.LP
The\fB\& if77\fR\fR\& command invokes the iPSC/860\& Fortran compiler, assembler, and linker with switches \
derived from the driver's command line switches. 
.LP
The\fB\& if77\fR\fR\& driver bases its processing on the suffixes of the files it is passed:
.IP "\(bu" 0.2i
Files whose names end with ``.F'' (uppercase) are considered to be Fortran programs. They are \
preprocessed (using the C preprocessor), compiled and assembled. The resulting object file is \
placed in the current directory. 
.IP "\(bu" 0.2i
Files whose names end with ``.f'' (lowercase) are considered to be Fortran programs. They are \
compiled and assembled. The resulting object file is placed in the current directory. 
.IP "\(bu" 0.2i
Files whose names end with ``.s'' are considered to be i860 assembly language files. They are \
assembled and the resulting object file is placed in the current directory. 
.IP "\(bu" 0.2i
Files whose names end with ``.o'' are considered to be object files. They are passed directly to \
the linker if linking is requested. 
.IP "\(bu" 0.2i
Files whose names end with ``.a'' are considered to be ar libraries. No action is performed on \
\&``.a'' files unless linking is requested. 
.IP "\(bu" 0.2i
All other files are taken as object files and passed to the linker (if linking is requested) with a \
warning message.
.LP
If a single Fortran program is compiled and linked with one \fB\
if77\fR\fR\& command, then the intermediate object and assembly files are deleted. 
.LP
The compiler is targeted to the Intel i860 System Binary Standard (SBS) for the Intel APX system. \
The compiler creates object files and executables that will function for any system conforming to the \
SBS. In addition, the compiler can create object files that will work for any system conforming to \
the System V, Release 4 Application Binary Interface (ABI) for the i860 microprocessor. 
.SH SWITCHES 
.LP
When debug or profile objects are compiled using the \fB\&-g\fR\fR\
\& or \fB\&-p\fR\fR\& switches, respectively, the same switch should be used when using \fB\
if77\fR\fR\& to link the objects. 
.LP
By default, Fortran local variables are placed on the stack. Some Fortran programs assume that all \
variables are allocated statically. Static allocation can be forced with the \fB\
\&-W0,-save\fR\fR\& switch. 
